From d09f9f07ba4889752af34fbad367e2ff6d89dc58 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Downstream Patch Queue <downstream@localhost>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 22:20:04 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 19/19] Synchronize the collector's price table daily

The headless collector priced every response from the bundled table and never
downloaded a newer one, so a host without a desktop client could stay on old
rates indefinitely.

PriceSync fetches the service relay's current table with libcurl using the
publisher's hardening (HTTPS only, no redirects, no proxy or netrc, verified
peer and host, short timeouts, no signals, 1 MiB cap), sends the stored ETag
and validates through the existing native PriceTable load. It rejects a
wrong schema as "newer schema", invalid tables, a table dated more than two
days after the local date and any version not strictly greater than the
current one; 304 and 200-with-same-version count as up to date. The table and
the {version, updated, url, etag, lastSuccessMs, lastAttemptMs, lastError,
failures} state are written to a temporary file and renamed under
~/.cache/agentspeed-collector/<digest>/, with the collector's existing
ownership and mode checks.

The sampler now owns one PriceBook for the daemon: the stored table is
restored at startup and a validated newer table is added to the running book,
so unbound days take the new rates and the shared analyzer reprises from the
parsed responses on its next scan without changing the persisted bindings.
The background round runs 60 s after start, daily after the last success,
retries once after an hour and re-checks hourly. Behavioural tests cover the
refusals, accepting a table without its own `tests`, atomic state round-trip
across a restart, ETag use and a newly priced model becoming known.
